Right.

3
00:00:07,660 --> 00:00:13,940
So, uh, the four functional layers basically give every component of the platform a definitive place.

4
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:19,800
So when planning this out, what is the, um, the very first move as an architect in your ministry?

5
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:23,280
Well, your first move is actually not to draw a new platform.

6
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:27,300
Right. It is, uh, to adopt a reference architecture.

7
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:31,460
By using the European framework and the X-Road experience,

8
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:35,660
your program can spend its scarce time on country-specific needs.

9
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:38,800
Instead of just re-deriving settled platforms.

10
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:42,620
Exactly. Instead of re-deriving settled interoperability platforms.

11
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:45,900
Okay, so what do these specific layers actually do?

12
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:48,640
Well, I'll, uh, I'll number them aloud in order.

13
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:49,160
Okay.

14
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:51,420
So one is service access.

15
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:55,600
This governs how a member system reaches the bus and calls a service.

16
00:00:56,060 --> 00:00:56,300
Mm-hmm.

17
00:00:57,320 --> 00:00:58,840
Two is event distribution.

18
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:01,400
Which dictates what exactly?

19
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:04,720
That dictates how messages and events move across the federation.

20
00:01:05,019 --> 00:01:05,459
Oh, okay.

21
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:09,120
Then three is the trust and security infrastructure.

22
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:11,080
Oh, like certificates and things?

23
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:14,340
Right. Certificates, identities, and, uh, mutual encryption.

24
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:17,680
And finally, four is governance and administration.

25
00:01:18,220 --> 00:01:20,000
Which covers the registries.

26
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:24,280
Yes. The registries of members and services and the platform monitoring.

27
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:32,700
So how does this structure settle practical questions like, um, where the service catalog or mutual encryption actually lives?

28
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:35,260
Well, the layers act as a filing system.

29
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:36,620
Ah, I see.

30
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:39,560
So the catalog is governance and administration.

31
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:43,140
Mutual encryption is trust and security infrastructure.

32
00:01:43,460 --> 00:01:43,620
Right.

33
00:01:44,380 --> 00:01:48,900
The reference architecture basically settles disagreements between architects by giving,

34
00:01:49,540 --> 00:01:52,380
you know, every component and standard a definitive home.

35
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:54,620
Which stops things from becoming fragmented.

36
00:01:54,800 --> 00:02:00,240
Exactly. It prevents systems from becoming fragmented, siloed, or duplicated across agencies.

37
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:04,220
So what is the deeper payoff for whole-of-government planning then?

38
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:08,740
A shared reference architecture is built once and reused by every sector.

39
00:02:09,479 --> 00:02:12,880
Successive agencies plug into the exact same four-layer shape.

40
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:14,580
Instead of inventing their own.

41
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:18,840
Right. You are reusing a settled structure instead of paying to discover it,

42
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:22,520
because without it, you end up locked into one supplier.

43
00:02:23,060 --> 00:02:24,340
And it becomes costly to change.

44
00:02:24,580 --> 00:02:26,240
Yes. Highly costly to change.

45
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:31,360
Now, looking at those layers, which one do countries often drop by accident?

46
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:34,080
It is almost always event distribution.

47
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:35,380
Really? Why is that?

48
00:02:35,980 --> 00:02:39,220
Well, request and response just asks a question and gets an answer.

49
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:43,780
But events tell everyone who cares, for example, that a birth was registered.

50
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:46,360
Oh, okay. That makes sense.

51
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:49,640
Right. And it is replayable if a subscriber is down.

52
00:02:50,120 --> 00:02:51,940
It achieves once-only its scale,

53
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:54,380
so it must be planned for deliberately.

54
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:56,400
Right. Not discovered later on.

55
00:02:56,800 --> 00:03:02,460
Exactly. Not discovered as a missing piece in year three when data is constantly being rekeyed.

56
00:03:02,860 --> 00:03:06,760
That would be a huge setback. So how do you apply this architecture practically?

57
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:11,840
You use the exercise of placing every component you build or buy into its layer.

58
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:14,260
So literally categorizing things.

59
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:20,480
Right. Your security server, the central registry, your monitoring tool, your identity adapter.

60
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:24,780
The sparse layers show exactly what your minister has not yet planned for.

61
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:27,100
Right. It makes the gaps obvious.

62
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:30,880
Exactly. Architecture starts with a borrowed proven structure.

63
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:35,020
Place every component into the four functional layers and read off the gaps.

64
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:40,360
You reuse the world's experience and keep your effort for what only your country can decide.

